<%# THE SCORING BANNER — one reused element, shared by every board that
    announces a score.

    Extracted from live/index.html.erb (unchanged) when the CONTEST live page
    adopted it. Its CSS already lived in live/_score_animations, so leaving the
    markup behind meant one page could style a banner it had no element for. The
    two travel together now.

    ONE element rather than one per event, so a rapid-fire sequence — a
    touchdown and its two-point conversion — REPLACES the banner instead of
    stacking a pile of them down the screen.

    NOT A RE-TIER — the ordering is PRESERVED, not changed. This overlay was
    z-[130] against a modal backdrop of z-[120]; it is now --z-alert (300)
    against --z-modal (200). Above the modal before, above the modal after. The
    number moved because the whole ladder moved; the relationship did not.

    That relationship is the point: a scoring banner a sticky header can paint
    over is a scoring banner nobody sees, which is exactly what a bare z-50
    bought before it was measured. It is pointer-events-none and self-dismisses,
    so sitting on top costs nothing — which is the whole case for --z-alert
    existing as a tier above --z-modal.

    ── THE HEAD COMES OFF THE TOP ────────────────────────────────────────────
    The scorer's portrait is bottom-aligned to the score line and taller than
    the row it sits in, so it breaks the banner's top edge the way a broadcast
    lower third does. TWO things make that possible and both are load-bearing:

      · The banner no longer clips. `overflow-hidden` moved DOWN onto the
        shimmer, which is the only child that actually needs it (a 45%-wide
        highlight travelling -120%→120% would otherwise paint over the page).
        Clipping at the banner meant the portrait was neatly guillotined at the
        rounded corner, which looked like a bug rather than a flourish.
      · The overlay carries top padding. The banner drops from the very top of
        the viewport, so the room the head needs above it has to exist BEFORE
        the head does. The overhang is the portrait's height minus the row's —
        1.75rem at phone size, 2.25rem above it — and pt-8 plus the banner's own
        m-3 gives 2.75rem, which clears both.

    THE PORTRAIT'S LANE IS RESERVED IN THE TEXT COLUMN, and the two numbers are
    tied: a 7rem-tall cutout from a 600x436 source draws about 9.6rem WIDE, so a
    lane sized to the height alone would have let the face sit on the words. The
    padding matches the cap, not the height.

    The portrait is positioned ABSOLUTELY rather than laid out in the row. In
    flow it would simply make the row as tall as itself, pushing the score line
    down and sticking off nothing. %>
<div id="nfl-score-overlay" class="fixed inset-x-0 top-0 pt-8 z-[var(--z-alert)] pointer-events-none hidden">
  <div id="nfl-score-banner" class="mx-auto max-w-lg m-3 rounded-xl shadow-2xl relative">
    <%# THE NAME BLOCK, in the team-card convention: the city quietly above, the
        ACTION below it in the team's own accent. It used to run the other way —
        a loud "FIELD GOAL" over a quiet "New England Patriots" — which spent
        the loud line on the event and then repeated the team in full. The city
        alone is enough to name a team whose colours are already the banner. %>
    <div class="relative flex items-center gap-4 px-5 pt-4 pb-3 min-h-[4.25rem] sm:min-h-[4.75rem]">
      <span id="nfl-score-emoji" class="text-4xl leading-none shrink-0"></span>
      <%# The right lane is RESERVED whether or not a face lands in it: the
          portrait and the points chip are alternatives in the same place, and a
          text column that reflowed between them would jog the wording on every
          banner that has no headshot. %>
      <div class="min-w-0 flex-1 pr-[8.5rem] sm:pr-[10rem]">
        <div id="nfl-score-team" class="text-sm font-semibold opacity-90 truncate leading-tight"></div>
        <div id="nfl-score-label" class="text-xl font-extrabold uppercase tracking-wide leading-tight truncate"></div>
      </div>
      <%# THE FACE, when we know them. The avatar REPLACES the points chip
          rather than joining it: "+6" is already implied by the word TOUCHDOWN
          above it, and a face is worth more than a number the reader can infer.
          Both slots stay in the markup — the league board raises this same
          banner from a feed that carries no scorer, and falls back to the
          points. %>
      <div id="nfl-score-points"
           class="absolute right-5 top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 text-3xl font-black tabular-nums opacity-95"></div>
      <%# bottom-0 is what "flush with the score line" means literally: the
          bottom of this box IS the top of the bar below. 7rem against a 4.75rem
          row leaves 2.25rem of head above the banner. %>
      <%# max-w is a WIDTH cap on a landscape source, not a crop: object-contain
          fits the cutout inside the box, so a narrow phone gets a slightly
          smaller portrait rather than one overlapping the words. %>
      <img id="nfl-score-avatar" alt=""
           class="absolute right-5 bottom-0 h-24 sm:h-28 w-auto max-w-[8.5rem] sm:max-w-[10rem]
                  object-contain object-bottom hidden">
    </div>

    <%# ── THE SCORE LINE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        THREE THINGS, and the score is the middle one. It used to be a single
        left-aligned string in a black wash, which put the one fact everybody is
        here for in the corner and left two-thirds of the bar empty.

        The bar is a 50/50 BLEND of the two teams' dark colours — away holding
        the left, home the right, meeting in the middle — which is the same
        recipe the split game tile and the contest chip use, so a score bar and
        a game card read as the same object. Each abbreviation is printed in its
        OWN team's light, which is why the blend is worth having: the colour
        under a team's name is that team's.

        Down and distance floats left of it and the quarter clock right, both
        blank on a game that is not in progress — so a FINAL collapses to just
        the score with the bar's colours intact.

        rounded-b-xl is not decoration here. The banner stopped clipping when
        the portrait began breaking its top edge, so this bar is now responsible
        for its own bottom corners. %>
    <div id="nfl-score-line" class="relative rounded-b-xl bg-black/25 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-bold">
      <div id="nfl-score-tally-row" class="grid grid-cols-3 items-center gap-2">
        <span id="nfl-score-situation" class="text-left truncate opacity-90"></span>
        <span id="nfl-score-tally" class="flex items-center justify-center gap-1.5 text-sm tabular-nums whitespace-nowrap">
          <span id="nfl-score-away-abbr"></span>
          <span id="nfl-score-away-pts"></span>
          <span class="opacity-50">–</span>
          <span id="nfl-score-home-pts"></span>
          <span id="nfl-score-home-abbr"></span>
        </span>
        <span id="nfl-score-clock" class="text-right truncate tabular-nums opacity-90"></span>
      </div>
      <%# THE RANK SUMMARY'S LINE. It wears this same banner but has one
          sentence to say, not a scoreline — and it used to say it by writing
          textContent onto this element, which with the three-column bar in
          place would delete the whole structure and leave a bare string. Its
          own slot, shown while the tally row hides. %>
      <div id="nfl-score-note" class="hidden text-center"></div>
    </div>

    <%# The shimmer is the ONLY child that clips. See the note above. %>
    <span id="nfl-score-shimmer" class="absolute inset-0 rounded-xl overflow-hidden pointer-events-none"></span>
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  // ── ONE PAINTER FOR TWO BOARDS ────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // The league board and the contest live page each drive this banner from
  // their own script, and each used to compose the score line itself — two
  // copies of one string, which is two copies that drift. The parts that depend
  // on the MARKUP above live here instead, next to the markup, so a change to
  // the bar is made once.
  //
  // Deliberately not a module and deliberately idempotent: both pages render
  // this partial exactly once, but a Turbo visit can re-evaluate it, and
  // re-assigning the same three functions costs nothing.
  window.NflBanner = {
    // The bar: the two teams' darks meeting in the middle, each abbreviation in
    // its own team's light, the situation either side of the tally.
    //
    // MISSING COLOURS FALL BACK TOGETHER, not field by field. A half-blended
    // bar — one team's navy against the CSS default — reads as a rendering
    // fault, so if either dark is absent the bar keeps its resting black wash
    // and only the text is painted.
    line: function (d) {
      var line = document.getElementById('nfl-score-line');
      if (!line) return;

      var awayDark  = d.awayDark, homeDark = d.homeDark;
      line.style.background = (awayDark && homeDark)
        ? 'linear-gradient(90deg, ' + awayDark + ' 0%, ' + awayDark + ' 34%, ' +
          homeDark + ' 66%, ' + homeDark + ' 100%)'
        : '';

      this.text('nfl-score-away-abbr', d.awayTeam || '', d.awayLight);
      this.text('nfl-score-home-abbr', d.homeTeam || '', d.homeLight);
      this.text('nfl-score-away-pts', d.awayScore || '0');
      this.text('nfl-score-home-pts', d.homeScore || '0');
      // Blank on anything that is not in progress, which is how a FINAL's bar
      // collapses to the score alone rather than freezing the last snap.
      this.text('nfl-score-situation', d.situation || '');
      this.text('nfl-score-clock', d.clock || '');

      this.mode('tally');
    },

    // The rank summary's one sentence, across the whole bar.
    note: function (message) {
      var line = document.getElementById('nfl-score-line');
      if (line) line.style.background = '';   // back to the resting wash

      // THE TALLY IS CLEARED, NOT MERELY HIDDEN. Hiding the row is what the
      // reader sees; clearing it is what keeps the two painters honest. The
      // overlay never blanks between a score and the summary that follows it
      // (see advanceChain), so anything left in a slot is INHERITED — and the
      // next thing to unhide this row would show the last game's numbers for a
      // frame before its own arrived. It is also what lets the render test
      // compare the two painters as SETS: every slot the score fills, the
      // summary accounts for.
      this.text('nfl-score-away-abbr', '');
      this.text('nfl-score-home-abbr', '');
      this.text('nfl-score-away-pts', '');
      this.text('nfl-score-home-pts', '');
      this.text('nfl-score-situation', '');
      this.text('nfl-score-clock', '');

      this.text('nfl-score-note', message || '');
      this.mode('note');
    },

    mode: function (which) {
      var tally = document.getElementById('nfl-score-tally-row');
      var note  = document.getElementById('nfl-score-note');
      if (tally) tally.classList.toggle('hidden', which !== 'tally');
      if (note)  note.classList.toggle('hidden', which !== 'note');
    },

    // The name block: the city quietly above, the action in the team's accent.
    // Colours arrive from the server's palette on the feed node; when one is
    // absent the line simply inherits the banner's own foreground, which is
    // what every banner looked like before the convention existed.
    name: function (location, action, locationColor, accentColor, accentShadow) {
      this.text('nfl-score-team', location || '', locationColor);
      this.text('nfl-score-label', action || '', accentColor);
      // THE HALO IS NOT OPTIONAL POLISH HERE. This banner's background is the
      // team's own card colour and this line is now the team's own mascot
      // colour — the pairing that measures 1.88:1 for Buffalo unaided. The tile
      // solves it with the same shadow; so does this.
      var label = document.getElementById('nfl-score-label');
      if (label) {
        var want = accentShadow || 'none';
        if (label.style.textShadow !== want) label.style.textShadow = want;
      }
    },

    text: function (id, value, color) {
      var el = document.getElementById(id);
      if (!el) return;
      if (el.textContent !== value) el.textContent = value;
      // An empty colour CLEARS the inline one rather than leaving the previous
      // team's on the element — the banner is reused, so the FINAL after a
      // touchdown would otherwise print in the scoring team's ink.
      var want = color || '';
      if (el.style.color !== want) el.style.color = want;
    }
  };
</script>
